{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>Shoham stones and milu'im stones for the ephod and for the choshen.</b> They are called avnei milu'im (setting stones) because one makes for them a setting in gold like a sort of socket (guma) and places the stone there to fill (lemalot) the socket. And the place of the socket is called mishbeẓet. <b>For the ephod and for the choshen:</b> the shoham for the stones of the ephod and the milu'im stones for the choshen — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Exodus 25:7) (below 28:20). And so he explained (there) \"moshbeẓim zahav yihyu bemilu'otam\" — surrounded by settings to a depth of the measure that will be filled by the thickness of the stone; this is the meaning of \"milu'otam,\" and likewise every expression of milui used in this context. But it is not correct in my eyes at all that He should call them now avnei milu'im on account of what He is yet to command to fill the sockets that they will make for them with them. Furthermore, the shoham stones too were set in gold settings, yet He did not call them milu'im. Furthermore, our Sages have already said in the Gemara (Sotah 48b): these stones are not cut with a knife, as it says \"bemilu'otam\" (below 28:20), and if the meaning of \"bemilu'otam\" were that the stones fill the sockets, it would be no proof from here at all that the names were not to be engraved on them with a knife. Furthermore, the matter of the setting (moshav) of the stones that the Rav said — that it is made like a sort of socket (guma) — is not so, but it is according to the words of Onkelos (Onkelos on Exodus 25:7) who translated \"meramẓan,\" which means that they make beneath it a seat the size of the stone, and from it they extend a prong (mazleg) of three teeth that hold the stone, in the language of the Sages: \"and he extracted it with a ramẓa of iron\" (Niddah 62a), like \"pierced with a ramẓa of iron\" (Shabbat 103a). And so they do even today with every precious stone in rings, so that it is visible from every side and its beauty and splendor are not hidden within the socket.\n\nYou can know that this is so, for two chains of gold that are threaded through the two rings on the choshen are fastened into the mishbeẓot on the shoulders of the ephod (Exodus 28:24ff.), and if the mishbeẓot are housings for the seating of stones, how would chains be fastened into them, and what use would the sockets within them serve there? Rather, they are prongs as we said, and the holes for the chains enter into them. And from this, in my opinion, comes the verse \"ki aḥazani ha-shavatz\" (II Samuel 1:9) — men with spears in hand and prongs (mizlagot) at their head to seize the fleeing, as he said (v. 6) \"and here the chariot and the horsemen were pressing after him.\" And the meaning of \"musabot mishbeẓot\" (Exodus 28:11) is that they make rimẓei zahav around. And according to Onkelos, who said \"meshak'in\" (there), the stones were set within the housings, and from them emerged rimẓin surrounding them and holding them from above.\n\nBut the meaning of \"milu'im\" is that the stones should be whole stones, created thus, and not hewn stones that were cut from a large quarry or from which anything was carved, for even in the nature of things it is known that there is no perfection of the powers of precious stones and their properties except in a stone that is like pieces of stones from the riverbed. Therefore Onkelos translated \"ashlamuta\" — for the expression milui of vessels or a socket in his translation is milui literally: \"vatemale kadah\" (Genesis 24:16) — \"umleiat\"; and so all of them — but here he translated milui as an expression of wholeness (shlemut). And so \"milei otam chochmat lev\" (Exodus 35:35) — \"ashlem,\" for it is not a thing that fills a vessel, but rather wholeness: that they were whole (shlemim) in wisdom. And this is the meaning of \"uvecharoshet even lemallot\" (Exodus 31:5), that they knew how to open seal-engravings on the stones in their milu'ot.\n\nNow regarding the stones of the ephod it says (Exodus 28:11) \"ma'aseh charash even pituchei chotam tefatach et shtei ha-avanim al shemot bnei Yisrael,\" for they would make a groove in them in the writing of the names as stone-engravers do — and these were not in their milu'ot, but regarding the stones of the choshen it is written (Exodus 28:17) \"umileita bo milu'at even,\" and it is written \"yihyu vemilu'otam\" (v. 20), and \"ha-avanim al shemot bnei Yisrael henah shtei esreh al shemotam\" (Exodus 39:14) — not \"ma'aseh charash.\" Therefore our master Moshe had no means for them other than the shamir that our Sages mentioned, as they said in tractate Sotah (48b): these stones are not written on with ink, as it says \"pituchei chotam\" (Exodus 28:21), and are not cut with a knife, as it says \"bemilu'otam\" (v. 20); rather, one brings the shamir and shows it to them and they split open on their own — and \"bemilu'otam\" is stated only regarding the stones of the choshen. And do not be troubled by what is mentioned in the Aggadah (Gittin 68a) that there is a shamir that Moshe brought for the stones of the ephod, for they would call the choshen an ephod by way of metonymy, because it is joined to it, and it is also written \"hagisha ha-ephod\" (I Samuel 23:9), and it was through the choshen that they would inquire. Now the meaning of the verse is: \"avnei shoham\" — three: two for the ephod and one for the choshen; \"ve'avnei milu'im\" — for the choshen. And if it were the opinion of our Sages that the stones of the ephod too were milu'im, then from this verse it would be: for the ephod and for the choshen, both of them — avnei shoham and avnei milu'im."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {0 "I resolved two abbreviated Aramaic phrases from the Gemara: 'ומפקי לה ברמצא דפרזלא' (Niddah 62a) as 'and he extracted it with a ramẓa of iron,' and 'דבזעא ברמצא דפרזלא' (Shabbat 103a) as 'pierced with a ramẓa of iron.' The verse in Exodus 28:24ff. is cited for the passage about the chains fastened into the mishbeẓot, though Ramban writes only the chapter reference (שמות כ\"ח)."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:16:44.370909603Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}